    "id": "fd_0130",
    "title": "Direction 1: Topological Restricted Products and Continuous Characters",
    "description": "**Conjecture:** The restricted product of locally compact abelian groups with compact open subgroups, endowed with the restricted product topology, is itself locally compact abelian, and every continuous homomorphism to \u2102\u02e3 trivial on the principal subgroup descends to a continuous homomorphism on the quotient (equipped with the quotient topology).\n\n**Test:** Formalize the restricted product topology in Lean 4 using Mathlib's `TopologicalSpace` and `TopologicalGroup` infrastructure. Verify that the topology on the restricted product (generated by basic opens of the form \"finitely many coordinates in open sets, remaining coordinates in the integral subgroup\") satisfies the `TopologicalGroup` axioms. Then prove that `QuotientGroup.lift` preserves continuity when the kernel is closed.\n\nA disproof would consist of an explicit counterexample: a restricted product where the lifted map fails to be continuous in the quotient topology.\n\n**Impact:** This would bridge the gap between our algebraic formalization and the analytic theory. Hecke characters are by definition *continuous* characters of the id\u00e8le class group; without topology, this constraint is invisible.\n\n**Catalog References:** `LanglandsGL1/RestrictedProduct.lean` (restrictedSubgroup), `LanglandsGL1/CharacterDescent.lean` (character_descends_to_idele_class_group)\n\n**Proof Strategy:** Use Mathlib's `TopologicalSpace.IsTopologicalBasis` to define the restricted product topology via basic opens. Prove local compactness via Tychonoff's theorem restricted to finitely many non-compact factors. For continuity of descent, use `QuotientMap.continuous_iff`.\n\n**Domain Bridges:** Topology, functional analysis, harmonic analysis (Pontryagin duality)\n\n**Lineage:** Extends `RestrictedProductData` with topological data; extends `character_descends_to_idele_class_group` with continuity.\n\n**Ambition:** \u2605\u2605\u2605\u2606\u2606 \u2014 Infrastructure-level; requires significant Mathlib topology work but is well-understood mathematically.\n\n---",
